{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-115716" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/a0b39135b78cefe8471c7d120850dde9b53786342679b5ed7f25af9110350607.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-x(x-3)$\n- ${y}^{′}=9-{x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)(x-3)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115716" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/a43217f2af81c1bbacbebd8800b7ebcd4076a32d6ff61782e1fdab4cd182d5ee.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=3,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-x(x-3)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=9-{x}^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not evaluate to zero at both $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)(x-3)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=3,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $x=2,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115716" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bcfbd4de4998f4ebbeff679485972944146bfb523c628e6b5e3fb63ea1a68cc3"} {"question" "q-115716" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115716" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115716" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-120025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/17cd72acbe8dd412d37c51ff992278d9664f6c5b1141a8604278fda76e57700f.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x-2$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x-2)$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x+1)(x-2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-120025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/5d6165488c8e41c16cf31b1b000a924344399c2b94a8fe5e7138c55adf97602b.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x-2$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not evaluate to zero at both $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x-2)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x+1)(x-2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=2,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $x=-1,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-120025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bcfbd4de4998f4ebbeff679485972944146bfb523c628e6b5e3fb63ea1a68cc3"} {"question" "q-120025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-120025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-120025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/36493e520e5f4dbc91482ab578ec4499eb00aee948171a6ea43d9d40c0e62938.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by ${y}^{′}=-(x-a)(x-b).$ Find the value of $a+b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/1da6182ac4999453b1c7e5f4c7db4d0a7e42f285463e8fe35aac5b8df46eb400.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-2$ and $x=4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-(x+2)(x-4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-2,b=4$ and $a+b=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bcfbd4de4998f4ebbeff679485972944146bfb523c628e6b5e3fb63ea1a68cc3"} {"question" "q-115714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115706" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/883a60e9ac0d526d76243a44cff129f04ca148c1c2f0fb6a4b34db630b2193cd.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by ${y}^{′}=(x-a)(x-b).$ Find the value of $a+b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115706" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/012fe788fa1ac778b361fe62c3f70344546c6c5008fa6dcce536cc721d4ba5e9.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-2$ and $x=1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x+2)(x-1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-2,b=1$ and $a+b=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115706" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bcfbd4de4998f4ebbeff679485972944146bfb523c628e6b5e3fb63ea1a68cc3"} {"question" "q-115706" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115706" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115706" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/170ddea2148f369d4feedd14540f234d7946bb6670d1ca6eb4c4ebbbb086a760.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=(x+3)(x+7)$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x+3)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(x+3)(x+5)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/cf77e09457cd6b53da801a6eb876a79712935eba623c851a5b6dafa58887a076.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-3$ and $x=-7,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(x+3)(x+7)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-7$ and $x=-3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x+3)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not evaluate to zero at $x=-7,$ and also evaluates to zero when $x=0,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(x+3)(x+5)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ but it also evaluates to zero when $x=-5,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bcfbd4de4998f4ebbeff679485972944146bfb523c628e6b5e3fb63ea1a68cc3"} {"question" "q-115719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-96095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/252d52b07c6c5e7a43c7816e3eb769e3f5abb561b84ef144ba0eeff022557260.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-96095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/dfd308fd43e27e7adaab6d40411cf33e53f0186faf5719c1ccbe9d934f552cca.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-2$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(x-1)(x+2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(x-1)(x+2) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(x-1)(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can dismiss the equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-1)(x+2).$ Although the right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $x=1,$ it also evaluates to zero when $x=0,$ which is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $x=1$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-2$ | $-2<x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x-1)(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=(x-1)(x+2)^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-96095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bcfbd4de4998f4ebbeff679485972944146bfb523c628e6b5e3fb63ea1a68cc3"} {"question" "q-96095" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-96095" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=(x+1)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=(x-1)(x+2)"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=(x-1)(x+2)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=(x-1)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=x(x-1)(x+2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-96095" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=(x-1)(x+2)^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115675" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/0d1dba03b8b14af89dbf06bf84f02c1a15f4f47f230900d4c46de87f14baeaf9.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115675" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/afb50fc9034d42ae57b9861dec4a2f5d0acf4f0c7122fc1ce845f5e3d60988fe.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=1$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-x)(x-4) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(1-x)(x-4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=1$ and $x=4$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<1$ | $1<x<4$ | $x>4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the three remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-x)(x-4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-x)(x-4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115675" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bcfbd4de4998f4ebbeff679485972944146bfb523c628e6b5e3fb63ea1a68cc3"} {"question" "q-115675" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115675" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=(1-x)(x-4)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=(x-4)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=2(4-x)"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-x)(x-4)"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115675" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-x)(x-4)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/b16e7007b3a266291fa22c1760e55e5b5e0a3e9a2d4ea97303f379a1f372cd32.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/d84c6743bd4cc1ab3306951f507f4691ea3c3f2075ead5f04328f64254d5a786.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(x+3) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(x+1)(x+3) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can dismiss the equation ${y}^{′}=x(x+1)(x+3).$ Although the right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ and $x=0,$ it also evaluates to zero when $x=-1,$ which is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-3$ and $x=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x(x+3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=x(x+3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bcfbd4de4998f4ebbeff679485972944146bfb523c628e6b5e3fb63ea1a68cc3"} {"question" "q-115677" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115677" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=x(x+3)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=(x+1)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=x(x+1)"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=x(x+1)(x+3)"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=x(x+3)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115677" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=x(x+3)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/a9c84c00dfdd0f84e8bf81dc4be8808794685a884f0e6bebd908da461e3de9a4.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/d7f1fccf2bb73f160faa04c058db6892f78f105e374516c97558f81121fc49f8.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}(2-x) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(x-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<0$ | $0<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the three remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}(2-x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(2-x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bcfbd4de4998f4ebbeff679485972944146bfb523c628e6b5e3fb63ea1a68cc3"} {"question" "q-115672" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115672" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=(x-2)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=-x(x+2)"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=x(x-2)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}(2-x)"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x+2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115672" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}(2-x)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-96094" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/76c15fa584f5a216d23ef10b10fbae14c09b84714e49bd8db0cc7954cab8c0f3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x-2$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-96094" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/111e679939475cabbfe9a40779de3fa1bf4f2de732f943a3b0396a3ee9bf3519.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<0$ | $0<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x-2$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=2$ only.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x<0$ | $0<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$ gives the required behavior.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-96094" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bcfbd4de4998f4ebbeff679485972944146bfb523c628e6b5e3fb63ea1a68cc3"} {"question" "q-96094" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-96094" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-96094" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115813" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/5a2a2b4b141f4f0c250e4865b174b9eb32b1ed4ab94cc6ebd074131673d5712c.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-2{x}^{2}-4x-2$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}+2x+1$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}-1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115813" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/69465ce0573d7b6deac631663766a5dd1c90afa29eeb0c5286cb5ef1f7ee1153.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the line $x=-1.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on this line.\n\n- Second, the line $x=-1$ divides the plane into $2$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $2$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2{x}^{2}-4x-2=-2(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+2x+1=(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-1=(x+1)(x-1)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115813" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bcfbd4de4998f4ebbeff679485972944146bfb523c628e6b5e3fb63ea1a68cc3"} {"question" "q-115813" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115813" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of those listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115813" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-120072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/6e57e25ea43f23e9d157b3fa1c621749d3d1a88bab389af66449c915d9e03606.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-120072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/523f18dc7b081ecf6dcfa1f09c6fe715012fffba3e4e07d1dfd44c3d53cf702c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x=-1$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(x+1),\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+x=x(x+1).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-1$ and $x=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-1$ | $-1<x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(x+1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-120072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bcfbd4de4998f4ebbeff679485972944146bfb523c628e6b5e3fb63ea1a68cc3"} {"question" "q-120072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-120072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=1-{x}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+x"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+1"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-120072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-120070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/51273365c4a282dc3dedd9d7cd5ac7d805e1526aaeee1915e8bd95d8223c26d3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}+3x$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}+x$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-120070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/a14651ffc883a8c24ad17bdd031658c3d4ae21db2cfded213f2ba911fdb9a765.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-3$ and $x=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+3x=x(x+3)\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+x=x(x+1)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1$ and $x=0.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(x+3)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ and $x=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-120070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bcfbd4de4998f4ebbeff679485972944146bfb523c628e6b5e3fb63ea1a68cc3"} {"question" "q-120070" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-120070" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-120070" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/b67f4648af37df7332a42a671fd8c3f7b0d7a8f626dc3ed9c4f32c1bb633f258.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/ef7114aaf0f2cc275b79fe312ac6e99bda2bf07667eaa4ccfaae3920538381b5.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}=2{x}^{2}(1-x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x=x(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $x=1$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions is easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<0$ | $0<x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bcfbd4de4998f4ebbeff679485972944146bfb523c628e6b5e3fb63ea1a68cc3"} {"question" "q-115846" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115846" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=(x-1)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=x(x+1)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=x+{x}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115846" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/b2af712f3b4cca278137bd358d0f889b9b2a63b3ae3d54d3c699b974f43d3a06.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/0034c86bbb1f4412cba2a7d202170b87fcd2570789a37affb88b399b085b33f6.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x=-1$ and $x=3.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x-3=(x+1)(x-3)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2(x+1)(x-3).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-1$ and $x=3$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions is easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-1$ | $-1<x<3$ | $x>3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bcfbd4de4998f4ebbeff679485972944146bfb523c628e6b5e3fb63ea1a68cc3"} {"question" "q-115850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=-2(x+1)(x-3)"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-1"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x-3"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-9"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+2x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-120076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/5fdb2ddd3d07537c1e3d0d22d435928f7cd11dc7a71c67f1bf45292c82a61ef7.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x+1)$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x+1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-120076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/cf2ba44d8cc21ade2204a3784af7d09b492843339a8b98c4cde741b30dfa54db.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=-1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x+1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not approach $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to -1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x+1}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}=\\frac{x}{(1+\\frac{1}{x})}\\to \\pm \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become vertical as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-120076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bcfbd4de4998f4ebbeff679485972944146bfb523c628e6b5e3fb63ea1a68cc3"} {"question" "q-120076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-120076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-120076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/2552222c6a0be1b1bf1e67a66b4906bb735c2d974d724d3e25e988d15388c696.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{8}{x+1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/eb1303132a4fe6a26c85b4733a3039a52109b4c49408e1c82038d71885d27f70.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=-1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side approaches $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to -1,$ but it also approaches $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to 1,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{8}{x+1}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{8}{x+1}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}=-\\frac{x}{(1+\\frac{1}{x})}\\to ∓\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bcfbd4de4998f4ebbeff679485972944146bfb523c628e6b5e3fb63ea1a68cc3"} {"question" "q-115751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/a355d9b35a7ebf791a56116b0ee5b127d0dfd90d6958b5bebd04aa9ad09a9ddc.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/6293d40f222d4d81236808f7ab0a35dfa82e1187b3a4d24bbc841a1b158f1039.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the solution curves have vertical asymptotes $x=-2$ and $x=1.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2-x-{x}^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{(x-1)(x+2)}\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+x-2}=\\frac{x}{(x-1)(x+2)}.\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $x=1$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-2$ | $-2<x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2-x-{x}^{2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bcfbd4de4998f4ebbeff679485972944146bfb523c628e6b5e3fb63ea1a68cc3"} {"question" "q-115853" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115853" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2-x-{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{x+2}{1-{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+x-2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115853" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2-x-{x}^{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-96098" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/680bc0001fb95783ee70fc88a7106997bcb3dba2ad85a708164878d0f610ecfb.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{5x}{x-2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x-2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-96098" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/74b482b5b72421c9ee208034dc4727605e42874015d080130ffd7d18437af0da.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=2.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not approach $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to 2.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{5x}{x-2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x}{x-2}=\\frac{5}{(1-\\frac{2}{x})}\\to 5\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x-2}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x-2}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-96098" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bcfbd4de4998f4ebbeff679485972944146bfb523c628e6b5e3fb63ea1a68cc3"} {"question" "q-96098" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-96098" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-96098" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115852" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/3e065edec23179a946ea28aa6f2b4f9a439ba23c014c38dded0cc7c756867a90.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115852" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/5b92aa0ecaf7dd906a39c44e31a12e725802d9c7c5cdc046351030ae04f6809e.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the solution curves have vertical asymptotes $x=-1$ and $x=2.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}=\\frac{1}{(x+1)(x-2)}\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-x-2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(x+1)(x-2)}.\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$ \n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}$ become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-x-2}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-x-2}$ do *not* become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115852" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bcfbd4de4998f4ebbeff679485972944146bfb523c628e6b5e3fb63ea1a68cc3"} {"question" "q-115852" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115852" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x-2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-x-2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "q-115852" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/state.json" "file_sha256" "97823b703ac104f2bc8dbfa8d8cd398090703f732e5fa7665460ea90b4cadc16"} {"question" "e-8483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/df8fd1746a9043aa52213a7ae5fd57b7f6c7438d02f5993c99bc8bd294309f07.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' = (x+1)(x-3)$\n\n- $y'= (x+1)(x-2)(x-3)$\n\n- $y' = x+1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/example-8483.json" "file_sha256" "e8882860b486f4e76b0148d7b6f6ea257ab5e5200f4a5e91365b33a309b843f5"} {"question" "e-8483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/180dafc23275d08fea5565296f3d72c92aee7ac2b4b68077b2c084d005a23456.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x = -1$ and $x = 3,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation $y'= (x+1)(x-3)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1$ and $x=3.$\n\n- The equation $y'=(x+1)(x-2)(x-3)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1$ and $x=3,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $x=2,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation $y' = x+1$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not evaluate to zero at both $x=-1$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/example-8483.json" "file_sha256" "e8882860b486f4e76b0148d7b6f6ea257ab5e5200f4a5e91365b33a309b843f5"} {"question" "e-8484" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/3ba95acb3d7d009a482f79e1224c0628ff43b2aa149df0918768c74576cc4d9a.png)\n\n \n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n- $y' = -x(x+4)^2$\n\n- $y' = -x^2(x+2)$\n\n- $y' = x^2(x+4)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/example-8484.json" "file_sha256" "8aa7f235b21956918cb05a2207ac57c4a66ac9838e29f9f500fe4a5523a11a89"} {"question" "e-8484" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/3fb7bf013703760852c86c2b16147b278fd1707931e980dbb693412832cdc5f1.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x = -4$ and $x = 0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-x(x+4)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}(x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-4$ and $x=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions is easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x <  -4$ | $-4 <  x <  0$ | $x > 0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\nearrow$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\searrow$ |\n| Sign | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\ny' = -x(x+4)^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y'= -x(x+4)^2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/example-8484.json" "file_sha256" "8aa7f235b21956918cb05a2207ac57c4a66ac9838e29f9f500fe4a5523a11a89"} {"question" "e-9312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/b2b67d39de238bceb50e0f21d65d99434b4a7f3be553a6c1c12876ecef3e5cf1.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' =x^2 - 4$\n\n- $y' = x^2 - 4x + 4$\n\n- $y' = 4 - x^2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/example-9312.json" "file_sha256" "4b5e942200e6b2908f97b60155a2aa0ed5319af01bbc8592317840551c144a74"} {"question" "e-9312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/b3768fbcd892e8484f0fabfca29bd6365cfc382bde2dd39c6ca8d278911747db.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x = -2$ and $x = 2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions is easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x <                  -2$ | $-2 <                  x <                  2$ | $x > 2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\nearrow$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| Slope | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny' =x^2 - 4 = (x+2)(x-2)\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\ny' = x^2 - 4x + 4 = (x-2)^2\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=2,$ but not at $x=-2.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny' = 4 - x^2 = (2+x)(2-x)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $x=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) does not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x <                  -2$ | $-2 <                  x <                  2$ | $x > 2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\searrow$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\searrow$ |\n| Sign | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/example-9312.json" "file_sha256" "4b5e942200e6b2908f97b60155a2aa0ed5319af01bbc8592317840551c144a74"} {"question" "e-8486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/49c8ba9f215b8ec05263c46eda9809728e918e94808023969c6b57d034470115.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{2}{(x+2)^2}$\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{1}{x+2}$\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{2x}{x+2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/example-8486.json" "file_sha256" "1c25663d4441144e22294994fc001db1bc574ed18deb6a4a1403a9c7a1a8c62e"} {"question" "e-8486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/65bedbf95e886a45884a914cab0100bacca6dc849606b15fe3a74ba7a4dd7dda.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=-2.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to\\pm\\infty.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $x>-2$ and negative for $x< -2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation $y' =  \\dfrac{2}{(x+2)^2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are positive for all $x \\neq  -2.$\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{1}{x+2}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $x>-2$ and negative for $x< -2,$ and $y'\\to 0$ as $x\\to\\pm\\infty.$\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{2x}{x+2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x}{x-2} = \\dfrac{2}{\\left(1-\\dfrac2x\\right)}\\to 2 \\quad\\text{as} \\quad x\\to \\pm\\infty.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $x\\to\\pm\\infty.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/example-8486.json" "file_sha256" "1c25663d4441144e22294994fc001db1bc574ed18deb6a4a1403a9c7a1a8c62e"}] "usage" {"q-96